WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.150
Module 15 is about precise context control.

2
00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:07.000
First up: rewindTo — stepping a chat back to an earlier point without leaving the chat you're in.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.578
Pick a point earlier in the conversation and rewind to it.

4
00:00:09.578 --> 00:00:17.000
Everything after that point is gone from the chat — not archived off to the side, actually removed — and you're back at that moment, ready to go a different direction.

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.747
Here's the safety net: rewindTo snapshots the chat before it deletes anything.

6
00:00:20.747 --> 00:00:26.079
So a rewind is itself reversible — if you rewind too far, or change your mind, you can get back what was there.

7
00:00:26.079 --> 00:00:28.000
It behaves like an undo, not a shredder.

8
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.454
Compare this to Branch from here, from episode forty-six.

9
00:00:30.454 --> 00:00:35.490
Branching preserves the original conversation and forks a brand new chat from that point — you end up with two chats.

10
00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:39.321
Rewind does the opposite: it mutates the current chat back to an earlier state, in place.

11
00:00:39.321 --> 00:00:41.000
Same starting idea, opposite trade-off.

12
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.699
Use rewind when you want a clean redo without a second chat cluttering your list.

13
00:00:44.699 --> 00:00:48.078
Use branch when you want to keep both paths side by side and compare them.

14
00:00:48.078 --> 00:00:51.000
They solve the same itch — a wrong turn — in two different ways.

15
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.134
Rewind operates on whole turns.

16
00:00:52.134 --> 00:00:57.000
Next episode goes finer-grained still: deleting a single reasoning block, tool call, or tool response without touching anything else.

